---
title: "The property tax dividend from Edmonton's RS Zone rowhomes"
date: "2026-07-12"
author: "Jacob Dawang"
categories: [housing, edmonton, zoning]
description: "Redeveloping an old house into a 5-8 unit RS Zone rowhome doesn't just add homes, it multiplies the property's assessed value. Here's what that's worth to Edmonton's property tax base since the Zoning Bylaw Renewal took effect."
link-external-newwindow: true
link-external-icon: false
execute:
  cache: false
draft: false
---

Edmonton's Zoning Bylaw Renewal (ZBR) has spent two and a half years adding new homes to existing neighbourhoods via the extremely successful 5-to-8-unit rowhome. [In an ongoing series of blog posts](../q2-building-permits/index.qmd), I track how the RS zone is slowly transforming our city. This post asks a different question: what is that redevelopment worth to the City's property tax base?

When a single-detached home in a mature neighbourhood becomes a RS Zone rowhome, its assessed value increases, adding to the City's property tax revenue.

Using building permits from 2024, the first year the RS Zone was in force, matched against the City's property assessment data, I measure that uplift directly for completed projects, then use a regression model fit on those measurements to estimate what it adds up to across the full 2024-2026 pipeline.

## Key facts

-   Mature-neighbourhood RS Zone rowhomes (5 to 8 units) will add an estimated **$1.3 billion** in assessed property value at steady state — once every 2024-2026 project is complete and fully reflected in an assessment roll — net of general market appreciation, across roughly **800** permitted redevelopment projects.

-   At 2026 municipal tax rates, that steady-state uplift will generate on the order of **$10-11 million** in property tax every year, a permanent addition to the tax base rather than a one-time bump.

-   Because most of that pipeline is still under construction or was only recently completed, the City has collected only about **$2 million** in additional property tax so far, across 2025 and 2026 combined — far below the eventual run rate.

-   No subsidy, grant, or City-led development produced this. Removing rules that made this kind of building illegal did.

## Data

This analysis combines three of Edmonton's Open Data sources. Edmonton's [general building permit data](https://data.edmonton.ca/Urban-Planning-Economy/General-Building-Permits/24uj-dj8v/about_data), the same source used in the [Q2 2026 building permit report](../q2-building-permits/index.qmd), identifies which properties were redeveloped into 5-8 unit RS Zone rowhomes and when. Edmonton's [current calendar year property assessment data](https://data.edmonton.ca/City-Administration/Property-Assessment-Data-Current-Calendar-Year-/q7d6-ambg/about_data) gives each property's assessed value as of 2026, and Edmonton's [historical property assessment data](https://data.edmonton.ca/City-Administration/Property-Assessment-Data-Historical-/qi6a-xuwt/about_data) gives assessed values back to 2012, including 2024 and 2025.




::: {.cell}

:::


## Measuring the uplift: the 2024 cohort

The 2026 property assessment is supposed to reflect each property's physical condition as of December 31, 2025. In principle, that means any 2024 permit whose building had received occupancy by the end of 2025 should be assessed as completed in the 2026 assessment roll. In practice, the assessments lag, as @sec-assessment-catch-up checks directly.


::: {.cell}

:::


Edmonton issued **118** mature-neighbourhood RS Zone 5-8 unit new-build permits in 2024, of which **79** had received occupancy by the end of 2025. I match all 118 to their assessment records, then use the occupancy dates to decide which ones the 2026 assessment actually reflects as complete.

### Matching permits to assessments

The key challenge is linking a specific building permit to the same physical property, before and after redevelopment. Account numbers usually persist through redevelopment, but addresses often don't: a lot can get re-addressed when it's rebuilt, so a permit's civic address is frequently not the address that appears in the property's earlier assessment record. Instead, I match on legal description (plan/block/lot), which is stable across redevelopment, with a fallback to geographic proximity for the small number of properties where the legal description format doesn't parse identically on both sides, or where redevelopment came with a brand new account number.

I parse each side's legal description into a `(plan, block, lot)` key and join on it. A building permit's legal description looks like `Plan 715HW Blk 25 Lot 22`; the historical assessment data records the same parcel as `Plan: 715HW  Block: 25  Lot: 22`.


::: {.cell}

:::


That legal-key match links most properties to their 2024 baseline assessed value directly. I close the gap two ways: a 25m geographic fallback for lots consolidated from two legal titles into one, and hand-matching for the handful of re-platted lots that geography can't reach either.


::: {.cell}

:::


The 25m fallback closes most of the remaining gap — in each case to the exact same civic address as the permit, at a matched distance of a few millimetres. The last 3 properties carry a legal description reissued under a brand-new subdivision plan and have no permit coordinates, so neither match reaches them. I hand-matched these by tracing each address through the historical assessment data; two had no 2024 assessment under either their old or new legal description, so I escalate their last observed 2023 value to a 2024 basis using the same median growth-ratio approach used elsewhere in this post. [I am not sure why I cannot find these two properties in the 2024 assessment roll. If you have any ideas, let me know. The addresses are 13009 - 109A AVENUE NW and 1, 11630 - 122 STREET NW]{.aside}


::: {.cell}

:::


Combining the geo-fallback with these three hand-matched properties links all **118** of 118 permits (100%) to a 2024 baseline assessed value. In most cases the account number persists through redevelopment, so I use it to look up the completed building's 2026 assessed value, again with a geographic fallback for the small number of properties issued a brand new account number when redeveloped.


::: {.cell}

:::


That yields **118** 2024 permits with both a 2024 baseline and a 2026 assessed value.

### When does the assessment catch up? {#sec-assessment-catch-up}

Before treating every occupancy-granted building as "complete" in the 2026 data, it's worth checking whether the assessments actually keep up with late-year completions. @fig-assessment-lag plots each matched property's gross uplift against its occupancy date. Buildings that received occupancy by roughly the end of September 2025 show the full rowhome uplift. For many October, November, and especially December completions, the uplift is much smaller and doesn't follow the same pattern — probably because their assessments still reflect an unfinished state.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Gross assessed value uplift by occupancy date: the 2026 assessment only reliably reflects buildings finished by about September 2025](index_files/figure-html/fig-assessment-lag-1.png){#fig-assessment-lag width=672}
:::
:::


The measurement cohort for this post is the matched 2024 permits with occupancy granted by September 30, 2025. Everything else, including those October-December completions, goes into the "not yet fully reflected" group.


::: {.cell}

:::


The measurement cohort is 48 properties. As an example, @tbl-spotlight traces two properties through the process, from their pre-redevelopment assessed value, through a mid-construction 2025 assessment, to their completed 2026 value. The Alberta Avenue example received occupancy in November 2025, yet its 2026 value looks fully assessed. This shows that the lag in @fig-assessment-lag is a trend, not a rule, which is why the conservative September cutoff is used for measurement.


::: {#tbl-spotlight .cell tbl-cap='Two properties traced from pre-redevelopment (2024) through mid-construction (2025) to a completed rowhome (2026)'}
::: {.cell-output-display}

