### A model for uplift {#sec-model-for-uplift}

The simple rules above ignore everything about a project except its unit count. But uplift plausibly varies with location too: land near LRT stations and in more expensive neighbourhoods could command different completed values. So alongside the flat medians, I fit a simple regression of each property's gross uplift on three things: units added, distance from the nearest LRT stop, and the neighbourhood's median RS-zone assessed value (a land-value measure computed from the same 2024 assessment data).

This isn't an attempt at a precise or causal model of what drives redevelopment value — with only 48 observations, it can't be, and that's not the point. It's a deliberately simple, "good enough" prediction rule, and the standard it needs to clear is agreement with the flat per-project medians above, with the payoff of a little more precision: a prediction tailored to each permit's own size and location rather than one number for every project of a given size. [Specification details — which covariates and transforms were tried, and how this model compares against simpler and more flexible alternatives by cross-validation — are in the @sec-model-appendix.]{.aside}


::: {.cell}

:::


As a sanity check against @tbl-uplift-rules: the model's average predicted uplift across the measured cohort is $1.7M per property, right in line with the simple medians, as it should be. What the model adds is the ability to give each individual permit a prediction tailored to its size and location, which matters when extrapolating to hundreds of permits whose size mix and geography differ from the measured cohort's.

For net uplift, the counterfactual adjustment is applied after prediction: a property's predicted net uplift is its predicted gross uplift minus (1.19 − 1) times its own 2024 baseline value, the same adjustment used for the measured cohort.

## From one cohort to the whole pipeline

The measured cohort only covers 2024 permits that were both matchable and fully reflected in the 2026 assessment. To estimate the cumulative effect of the RS Zone rowhome since ZBR took effect, I apply the uplift model (coefficients in @tbl-uplift-model, appendix) to every other mature-neighbourhood RS Zone 5-8 unit new-build permit issued since 2024: the rest of the 2024 cohort (permits that didn't match or aren't fully assessed yet), all of 2025, and 2026. Each permit gets a prediction from its own unit count, LRT distance, and neighbourhood.

This is a **steady-state estimate**: it assumes every one of those permits eventually reaches the completed-building uplift the model predicts, once construction wraps up and a future assessment catches up to it. It is not the smaller amount those properties currently show in the 2026 roll, which @tbl-2024-actual-total showed directly for 2024's not-yet-reflected permits. The gap between that actual, partial total and the steady-state estimate below is construction and assessment lag, not double-counting.


::: {.cell}

:::


2025 permitted **302** more mature-neighbourhood RS Zone 5-8 unit rowhomes, and H1 2026 has already permitted 189. Following the same [H1 annualization method used in the Q2 2026 report](../q2-building-permits/index.qmd), full-year 2026 is projected at 2.1x the H1 count.

Predicting net uplift needs each permit's own 2024 baseline value, so the 2025 and 2026 permits are run through the same baseline matchers as the 2024 cohort (their lots all existed in the 2024 roll, pre-redevelopment).


::: {.cell}

:::


The 2025 and 2026 permits match their 2024 baselines at a **96%** rate; the few that don't use their neighbourhood's median RS value as a stand-in baseline for the net adjustment.

@fig-cumulative-value-uplift shows the resulting cumulative assessed value uplift by permit year, split between the actually-measured portion and the portion estimated with the model.


::: {.cell .preview-image}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Cumulative estimated assessed value uplift since ZBR, by permit year (net of appreciation)](index_files/figure-html/fig-cumulative-value-uplift-1.png){#fig-cumulative-value-uplift width=672}
:::
:::


Adding it all up: since ZBR took effect, mature-neighbourhood RS Zone 5-8 unit rowhomes are estimated to have added **$1.4B** in gross assessed value, or **$1.3B** net of general market appreciation, across 118 (2024) + 302 (2025) + 397 (2026, projected) = 817 permitted projects.



## What this means for property tax

Assessed value only matters for the City's budget insofar as it's taxed. I convert the estimated uplift into two figures: an **annual recurring** estimate, at 2026 municipal tax rates, of how much more property tax this pipeline will generate every year once every project is complete and fully reflected in an assessment roll; and a rougher **cumulative collected so far** estimate that accounts for the fact that most of this pipeline is still under construction or only recently completed.

Completed 5-8 unit rowhomes on a single title are typically assessed as "Other Residential", which carries a higher municipal mill rate than the "Residential" class the original house was assessed under, but that split isn't uniform: in the measured cohort, essentially every 7-8 unit building lands in Other Residential, while most 5-6 unit buildings stay Residential. In the measured cohort overall, 83% of completed properties land in Other Residential; for the model-based portion of the estimate, I apply that same mix separately within the 5-6 and 7-8 unit buckets, rather than one flat rate for every permit regardless of size.


::: {.cell}

:::


At 2026 municipal tax rates, this pipeline is estimated to add **$10.8M** in property tax every year, net of what general appreciation would have generated anyway ($11.2M on a gross basis). That's before counting the provincial education levy, which would add roughly another third on top of the municipal portion.

Because most of this pipeline is still ramping up, actual tax collected so far is much lower than that steady-state figure. Comparing each matched property's mid-construction 2025 assessed value to its 2024 baseline and completed 2026 value shows how much of the total uplift typically shows up one year after a permit is issued.


::: {.cell}

:::


The median property has captured about 13% of its full uplift by the assessment one year after permitting, consistent with most projects still being under construction at that point. Modelling each year's cohort as contributing that partial share in the following year and the full amount from two years out, I estimate Edmonton has collected roughly **$2.3M** in incremental property tax from this pipeline across 2025 and 2026 combined, well short of the $10.8M-a-year steady-state run rate established above.

These are estimates, not City budget figures. Assessment growth from new construction doesn't translate one-for-one into new City revenue, since Council sets the mill rate each year based on total budget needs, but new construction is genuinely additional tax base at whatever rate Council sets, distinct from a rate increase on existing properties. This also doesn't account for the cost of servicing new residents, which isn't the subject of this post.

## Where is this happening?

That citywide total is the sum of hundreds of individual redevelopments, concentrated unevenly across mature Edmonton. @tbl-cumulative-selected-neighbourhoods breaks the cumulative estimate down for the same neighbourhoods used in the [Q2 2026 report's selected-neighbourhood table](../q2-building-permits/index.qmd), places that have been prominent in debates about RS Zone infill. Each permit is valued at its actual measured uplift where available, or the model's prediction otherwise, then summed by neighbourhood. This covers every actual permit issued through H1 2026; the projected remainder of 2026 isn't tied to real permits yet, so it's included in the citywide total above but left out of this neighbourhood breakdown.


::: {#tbl-cumulative-selected-neighbourhoods .cell tbl-cap='Cumulative estimated uplift for selected neighbourhoods, 2024 through H1 2026'}
::: {.cell-output-display}

