## Conclusions

The RS Zone rowhome isn't just adding homes, it's rebuilding the property tax base of neighbourhoods that had been losing population and, with it, tax revenue per hectare of city-maintained infrastructure for decades. A handful of redevelopments per neighbourhood, concentrated in mature areas that already have the pipes, roads, and schools in place, is already worth more than $10.8M a year in additional municipal property tax capacity at steady state, on top of the housing it delivers.

None of this required a subsidy, a grant, or a City-led development. It required removing rules that made this kind of building illegal. If you'd like to help keep it that way, consider joining [Grow Together Edmonton](https://www.growtogetheryeg.com/).

## Appendix: model specification details {#sec-model-appendix}

The regression used to extrapolate uplift across the pipeline ([above](#sec-model-for-uplift)) is a deliberately simple prediction rule, not a causal model — its job is to agree with the flat per-project medians while adding a bit of size- and location-specific precision. This appendix documents the two specification choices behind it, for readers who want the detail.

First, modelling gross uplift is algebraically the same as modelling the completed 2026 value with the 2024 baseline as a fixed offset. Letting the baseline enter with a freely estimated coefficient instead doesn't help: the old house's value is nearly collinear with the neighbourhood land-value measure (r = 0.88), so the offset form is both more stable and better in cross-validation.

Second, the uplift itself is *not* log-transformed: the measured cohort's uplift distribution isn't right-skewed enough to justify it, and a log-log variant does worse in cross-validation (see @tbl-model-comparison). But its two *covariates* are worth transforming: I searched units added and neighbourhood value on both linear and log scales, and distance from LRT on both linear and inverse-distance scales, always keeping gross uplift itself on the dollar scale, evaluated by leave-one-out cross-validation on the dollar scale. @tbl-model-comparison shows the two ends of that search. Log-transforming units added and neighbourhood value together gives the best cross-validated fit, an improvement over the plain linear specification and clearly ahead of log-transforming the outcome instead. With only 48 observations, that improvement is real but not dramatic — about 1.5 times its own bootstrap standard error, so I adopt it as the best available specification on the evidence, not because the gap is large.


